import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Best Portable Power Stations for Blackouts and Camping — DealWillow"
  description="The best portable power stations in the US right now: the Anker Solix C1000 V2 for most people, the SOLIX S2000 for the smallest 2kWh home backup, and the Jackery Explorer 300 for grab-and-go."
>
  <main class="wrap article">
    <a class="crumb" href="/">&larr; All reviews</a>

    <div class="head">
      <span class="cat-tag">Power</span>
      <h1>Best Portable Power Stations for Blackouts and Camping</h1>
      <p class="meta">Updated Nov 2026 · Prices checked Nov 9, 2026 · US models only</p>
    </div>

    <div class="lede">
      <p>
        <strong>For most people, the Anker Solix C1000 V2 is the one to buy.</strong> It's GearJunkie's
        2026 best-overall pick at 9.4/10, charges from wall to full in about 49 minutes, and its
        1,024&nbsp;Wh is the sweet spot for camping weekends and short outages. If your real worry is
        a multi-day blackout at home, the <strong>SOLIX S2000</strong> crams 2&nbsp;kWh into the
        smallest body on the market. If you just want a toaster-sized battery for tailgates and
        phone-charging emergencies, that's the <strong>Jackery Explorer 300</strong>.
      </p>
    </div>


    <h2 class="block-title">Everything we recommend</h2>
    <div class="picks">
      <a class="pick" href="#c1000">
        <span class="pick-tag">Best overall</span>
        <span class="pick-role">1 kWh, 49-minute recharge, 5-year warranty</span>
        <span class="pick-name">Anker Solix C1000 V2</span>
        <span class="pick-price">~$550 · Amazon</span>
      </a>
      <a class="pick" href="#s2000">
        <span class="pick-tag alt">Best 2 kWh backup</span>
        <span class="pick-role">Smallest and lightest in its class</span>
        <span class="pick-name">Anker SOLIX S2000</span>
        <span class="pick-price">$749.99 · Amazon</span>
      </a>
      <a class="pick" href="#jackery">
        <span class="pick-tag">Best grab-and-go</span>
        <span class="pick-role">Toaster-sized, keeps charge for years</span>
        <span class="pick-name">Jackery Explorer 300</span>
        <span class="pick-price">~$300 · Amazon</span>
      </a>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ 1. C1000 V2 ============ -->
    <section class="product" id="c1000">
      <h2 class="prod-role">Best overall</h2>
      <h3 class="prod-name">1. Anker Solix C1000 V2</h3>

      <div class="specs">
        <div><span>Capacity</span><b>1,024 Wh · LiFePO4</b></div>
        <div><span>Output</span><b>2,800 W (3,000 W surge)</b></div>
        <div><span>Recharge</span><b>~49 min from wall (UltraFast)</b></div>
        <div><span>Ports</span><b>6 AC · 2 USB-C · 2 USB-A · 1 car</b></div>
        <div><span>Weight</span><b>25 lb</b></div>
        <div><span>Warranty</span><b>5 years</b></div>
      </div>

      <div class="proscons">
        <div class="pros">
          <p class="pc-title">Reasons to buy</p>
          <ul>
            <li>GearJunkie's 2026 best overall (9.4/10) after field testing</li>
            <li>Wall-to-full in about 49 minutes — fastest in its class</li>
            <li>Large 2,800 W inverter for its size handles coffee makers and Instant Pots</li>
            <li>5-year warranty beats most rivals</li>
            <li>Light enough (25 lb) to actually carry on a camping trip</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="cons">
          <p class="pc-title">Reasons to avoid</p>
          <ul>
            <li>The V2 dropped the expansion port of the previous version</li>
            <li>1 kWh won't run a fridge for days — that's the S2000's job</li>
            <li>No longer includes the light bar of the original C1000</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="body">
        <p>
          The C1000 sits at the center of the category for a reason: 1,024&nbsp;Wh covers a weekend of
          camping or a few hours of a blackout for essentials, and at 25&nbsp;lb it's still one-hand
          carryable — which, in this category, is the difference between a tool you use and a brick
          you store. GearJunkie's field testers rate it 9.4/10 for 2026, and the specs back that up:
          a 2,800&nbsp;W inverter (3,000&nbsp;W surge), six grounded AC outlets, two USB-C and two
          USB-A, and LiFePO4 cells behind a 5-year warranty.
        </p>
        <p>
          The headline feature is charging speed. Wirecutter's earlier measurements of the C1000 line
          found it kept a fan running for 9 hours and hit 1,847&nbsp;W of measured output against an
          1,800&nbsp;W rating — numbers that match how the V2 behaves at the outlet: Anker rates the
          UltraFast charge at roughly 49 minutes to full. If the grid is up, you can top this thing up
          during lunch.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two things changed in the V2 and you should know both: it lost the previous model's expansion
          battery port, and it no longer has the light bar. Neither matters much for most buyers —
          the added 2,000&nbsp;W inverter and the weight loss matter more — but if you were counting on
          expanding to 2&nbsp;kWh later, buy the S2000 instead.
        </p>
        <p><strong>The bottom line:</strong> buy once, cry once — this is the model that covers the most situations with the fewest compromises.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ 2. S2000 ============ -->
    <section class="product" id="s2000">
      <h2 class="prod-role">Best 2 kWh backup</h2>
      <h3 class="prod-name">2. Anker SOLIX S2000</h3>

      <div class="specs">
        <div><span>Capacity</span><b>2,010 Wh · 314Ah LFP cells</b></div>
        <div><span>Output</span><b>1,500 W rated (3,000 W peak)</b></div>
        <div><span>Cycle life</span><b>10,000 cycles to 60% (claimed)</b></div>
        <div><span>UPS switchover</span><b>≤10 ms</b></div>
        <div><span>Size / weight</span><b>8.2 × 11.1 × 12.7 in · 35.7 lb</b></div>
        <div><span>Expandable</span><b>No</b></div>
        <div><span>Noise</span><b>30 dB</b></div>
        <div><span>Warranty</span><b>5 years</b></div>
      </div>

      <div class="proscons">
        <div class="pros">
          <p class="pc-title">Reasons to buy</p>
          <ul>
            <li>The smallest and lightest 2 kWh station sold right now</li>
            <li>10,000 cycles to 60% — the longest-rated battery in this class</li>
            <li>10 ms UPS switchover keeps routers and NAS boxes alive through outages</li>
            <li>30 dB quiet — quieter than a whisper at normal loads</li>
            <li>For its size, 35 hours of fridge runtime per Anker's own lab numbers</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="cons">
          <p class="pc-title">Reasons to avoid</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Only 1,500 W rated output — high-draw appliances can trip it</li>
            <li>Not expandable; the C2000 Gen 2 is the model that grows</li>
            <li>400 W max solar input is modest for off-grid use</li>
            <li>No 12 V DC car port (the C2000 has one)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="body">
        <p>
          The S2000's entire pitch is the body: 2&nbsp;kWh of storage in something the size of a
          carry-on bag, at 35.7&nbsp;lb — Anker says that's the smallest and lightest in the class, and
          no other 2&nbsp;kWh unit we found contradicts it. For a home backup that lives in a closet
          and only comes out when the power dies, that size is the point.
        </p>
        <p>
          The other stat that stands out is longevity: 314Ah LFP cells rated for 10,000 cycles to 60%
          capacity, which Anker frames as a 15-year lifespan at 1–2 cycles a day. Cycle ratings are
          lab-derived and yours will vary — but a rating double the typical 5,000 is still a meaningful
          difference in total cost of ownership.
        </p>
        <p>
          The trade-offs are about output, not quality. At 1,500&nbsp;W rated, it will run a fridge,
          a router, lights, a TV and a laptop indefinitely, but a space heater or a full-size
          microwave will make it unhappy — the C2000 Gen 2 (2,400&nbsp;W) is the one for RV
          air-conditioners and tools. And unlike the C2000, the S2000 can't take an expansion battery,
          so you're buying exactly 2&nbsp;kWh, forever.
        </p>
        <p><strong>The bottom line:</strong> the best pick if whole-home essentials matter more than peak wattage — and you want to carry it solo.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ 3. Jackery ============ -->
    <section class="product" id="jackery">
      <h2 class="prod-role">Best grab-and-go</h2>
      <h3 class="prod-name">3. Jackery Explorer 300</h3>

      <div class="specs">
        <div><span>Type</span><b>Small Li-ion station (~300 Wh class)</b></div>
        <div><span>Size</span><b>"About the size of a toaster" per Wirecutter's tester</b></div>
        <div><span>Use case</span><b>Phones, laptops, air mattress, lights</b></div>
        <div><span>Solar</span><b>Pairs with Jackery's SolarSaga panels</b></div>
        <div><span>Longevity</span><b>Held 69% charge after a year in a garage (tested)</b></div>
      </div>

      <div class="proscons">
        <div class="pros">
          <p class="pc-title">Reasons to buy</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Long-term-tested by Wirecutter: two years of use, still holds charge well</li>
            <li>Small enough for a car trunk, suitcase, or closet shelf</li>
            <li>Simple enough that nobody needs a manual</li>
            <li>Solar-panel add-on works when the grid is down</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="cons">
          <p class="pc-title">Reasons to avoid</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Nowhere near enough capacity to run a fridge or a space heater</li>
            <li>Shorter runtimes than any 1 kWh+ station</li>
            <li>No 2,000 W-class appliances — it's for small electronics</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="body">
        <p>
          Wirecutter's tester bought hers for wildfire season and has used it for two years; after
          sitting in a garage for over a year, the unit still held a 69% charge. That's the real test
          for a backup battery — whether it's alive when you need it — and the Explorer 300 passes it.
          It's roughly toaster-sized, which sounds like a joke until you try stuffing a 25-lb power
          station into a sedan next to camping gear.
        </p>
        <p>
          Be clear about what it is: a phone-charging, air-mattress-inflating, lamp-running box for
          tailgates and short outages. It will not carry a fridge, and that's fine — that's what the
          two Ankers above are for.
        </p>
        <p><strong>The bottom line:</strong> the one to keep in the trunk, not the one to plan a blackout around.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="sec">
      <h2 class="block-title">Also worth knowing</h2>
      <p>
        Two more names come up repeatedly in 2026 testing: the <strong>EcoFlow Delta 3 Ultra
        Plus</strong> (GearJunkie's best home-backup pick) and the <strong>Bluetti AC180</strong>
        (repeatedly cited as the cheapest way into 1&nbsp;kWh at ~$499). We haven't cross-checked
        either against primary lab data for this guide, so treat them as leads — worth a look if
        neither Anker fits your budget or ports.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="sec">
      <h2 class="block-title">How we picked</h2>
      <p>
        We started from Wirecutter's and GearJunkie's field testing, then verified every spec against
        Anker's own US product pages — capacity, inverter rating, cycle life, weight, warranty.
        Prices were checked on <strong>Nov 9, 2026</strong>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="sec">
      <h2 class="block-title">Before you buy: two things almost nobody tells you</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Output wattage matters more than capacity for appliances.</strong> A 2 kWh station
        with a 1,500 W inverter will refuse to start a space heater that a 1 kWh station with a
        2,800 W inverter handles fine. Check the startup surge on whatever you plan to run — motors
        and heaters pull 2–3× their label wattage for a second.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Store it at 50–80%, not 100%.</strong> Lithium packs age fastest at full charge and
        at zero. For an emergency-only unit, top it to about 80% and check it every few months — that's
        the single habit that most extends the battery's useful life.
      </p>
    </section>

